---
title: Feature Flag Configuration Overview
---

The Android app's feature flags live in a single JSON file in your local clone: **`config/app-config.json`**. This is the file you'll edit to turn features on or off without touching any Kotlin source code.

## Where It Lives

{% filetree %}

- config/
  - app-config.json   ← Feature flags live here
  - theme.json
- app/
- build-tools/
- vonage-feature-chat/
- vonage-feature-captions/
- vonage-feature-screensharing/
- ...

{% /filetree %}

## How It's Structured

`app-config.json` also carries the backend URL placeholder alongside four feature-flag sections:

```json
{
  "baseApiUrl": "${BASE_API_URL}",
  "videoSettings": {
    "allowBackgroundEffects": true,
    "allowCameraControl": true,
    "allowVideoOnJoin": true,
    "defaultResolution": "640x480"
  },
  "audioSettings": {
    "allowAdvancedNoiseSuppression": true,
    "allowAudioOnJoin": true,
    "allowMicrophoneControl": true
  },
  "waitingRoomSettings": {
    "allowDeviceSelection": true
  },
  "meetingRoomSettings": {
    "allowSettings": true,
    "allowArchiving": true,
    "allowCaptions": true,
    "allowChat": true,
    "allowDeviceSelection": true,
    "allowEmojis": true,
    "allowScreenShare": true,
    "defaultLayoutMode": "activespeaker",
    "showParticipantList": true
  }
}
```

- **`baseApiUrl`** — the backend URL placeholder you configured in the previous step (leave this alone; it's substituted from `local.properties` or the `BASE_API_URL` environment variable).
- **`videoSettings`** — controls camera behavior and video quality before and during a call.
- **`audioSettings`** — controls microphone behavior and audio settings.
- **`waitingRoomSettings`** — controls what participants can do while waiting to be admitted.
- **`meetingRoomSettings`** — controls in-call features once a participant has joined the meeting room.

## Representative Flags

Here's a closer look at some of the most commonly changed flags:

| Flag | Section | Type | Default | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `allowChat` | `meetingRoomSettings` | boolean | `true` | Shows or hides the in-call chat panel |
| `allowArchiving` | `meetingRoomSettings` | boolean | `true` | Enables or disables session recording (archiving) controls |
| `allowCaptions` | `meetingRoomSettings` | boolean | `true` | Enables or disables live captions during a call |
| `allowEmojis` | `meetingRoomSettings` | boolean | `true` | Enables or disables the emoji reaction picker |
| `allowScreenShare` | `meetingRoomSettings` | boolean | `true` | Enables or disables the screen-share button |
| `allowBackgroundEffects` | `videoSettings` | boolean | `true` | Enables or disables virtual background/blur effects |
| `defaultResolution` | `videoSettings` | string | `"640x480"` | Sets the default camera capture resolution used when a call starts |
| `defaultLayoutMode` | `meetingRoomSettings` | string | `"activespeaker"` | Sets the default video layout (e.g. active speaker vs. grid) when a meeting room loads |

## How Flags Reach the App

The custom `JsonConfigPlugin` Gradle plugin reads `app-config.json` at build time and generates two things:

| Output | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `AppConfig.kt` | Runtime-accessible Kotlin constants (e.g. `AppConfig.MeetingRoomSettings.ALLOW_CHAT`) |
| `gradle/generated-config.properties` | Build properties used to select product flavor variants for optional feature modules (e.g. `vonage-feature-chat`) |

{% aside %}
Some features, like chat, are entire optional Gradle modules with `enabled`/`disabled` product flavor variants. The correct variant is selected automatically based on your `app-config.json` values — you never need to pick a flavor manually.
{% /aside %}

Next, you'll edit some of these flags and regenerate the corresponding Kotlin configuration in your local clone.
